# Why This Matters for the SoC

A complete SoC is essentially a collection of hardware components connected together.

For example:


                         CPU
                          │
                          ▼
                   ┌───────────┐
                   │   Bus     │
                   └─────┬─────┘
                         │
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          ▼              ▼              ▼
         RAM            GPIO           UART
          │              │              │
          │              ▼              ▼
          │             Pins          Serial
          │
          ▼
        Memory


The CPU can control these components through registers.

This is a fundamental concept in SoC design.

---

# Future FPGA Application

When this project is eventually moved onto an FPGA board, the GPIO output can be connected to actual FPGA pins.

For example:


CPU
 │
 ▼
Wishbone
 │
 ▼
GPIO Register
 │
 ▼
FPGA Pin
 │
 ▼
LED


Then a program running on the CPU could potentially control the LED by writing to the GPIO register.

For example:


GPIO = 1


could turn an LED on.

And:


GPIO = 0


could turn it off.

This is the connection between the digital logic being simulated now and the physical FPGA hardware that will be used later.

